Avro v2 (add warehouse — must have default for backward compat) — inventory_v2.avsc:

```json
{
  "type": "record", "name": "InventoryItem",
  "fields": [
    {"name": "itemName", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "quantity", "type": ["null", "int"], "default": null},
    {"name": "colors", "type": {"type": "array", "items": "string"}},
    {"name": "warehouse", "type": ["null", "string"], "default": null}
  ]
}
```


Reader v2 decoding v1-written record outputs:
```json
{"itemName": "Lamp", "quantity": 3, "colors": ["red", "blue"], "warehouse": null}
```
When a reader expects a field absent from the writer schema, Avro supplies the default declared by the reader schema.

Reverse translation JSON → protobuf: assign fresh tag numbers, never infer tags from JSON key order. JSON → Avro: map JSON types to Avro primitives; use union with null + default for optional fields.

Comment: `protoc --encode=InventoryItem` and `avro-tools jsontofrag` are the CLI checks; Confluent Schema Registry stores the Avro v2 JSON and enforces that `warehouse` has a default before registration.

Install the trigger before starting the backfill so concurrent inserts and name changes immediately receive the stored value. The temporary `NOT VALID` check protects new writes without scanning historical rows.

Save the completed `ch5_normalized_name.sql`:

```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS ch5_projects CASCADE;
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS ch5_backfill_normalized_names(integer);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS ch5_fill_normalized_name();
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS ch5_normalize_name(text);

CREATE FUNCTION ch5_normalize_name(p_name text)
RETURNS text
LANGUAGE sql
IMMUTABLE
STRICT
AS $$
  SELECT lower(btrim(p_name));
$$;

CREATE TABLE ch5_projects (
  id bigint PRIMARY KEY,
  name text NOT NULL
);

INSERT INTO ch5_projects (id, name)
SELECT g, '  Project ' || g || '  '
FROM generate_series(1, 20000) AS g;

ALTER TABLE ch5_projects ADD COLUMN normalized_name text;

CREATE FUNCTION ch5_fill_normalized_name()
RETURNS trigger
LANGUAGE plpgsql
AS $$
BEGIN
  NEW.normalized_name := ch5_normalize_name(NEW.name);
  RETURN NEW;
END;
$$;

CREATE TRIGGER ch5_projects_fill_normalized_name
BEFORE INSERT OR UPDATE OF name ON ch5_projects
FOR EACH ROW
EXECUTE FUNCTION ch5_fill_normalized_name();

ALTER TABLE ch5_projects
ADD CONSTRAINT ch5_projects_normalized_name_nn
CHECK (normalized_name IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID;

CREATE FUNCTION ch5_backfill_normalized_names(
  p_batch_size integer DEFAULT 2000
)
RETURNS integer
LANGUAGE plpgsql
AS $$
DECLARE
  v_rows integer;
BEGIN
  WITH batch AS (
    SELECT id
    FROM ch5_projects
    WHERE normalized_name IS NULL
    ORDER BY id
    LIMIT p_batch_size
    FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED
  )
  UPDATE ch5_projects AS p
  SET normalized_name = ch5_normalize_name(p.name)
  FROM batch
  WHERE p.id = batch.id;

  GET DIAGNOSTICS v_rows = ROW_COUNT;
  RETURN v_rows;
END;
$$;

SELECT count(*) AS rows_to_backfill
FROM ch5_projects
WHERE normalized_name IS NULL;

INSERT INTO ch5_projects (id, name)
VALUES (20001, '  New PROJECT  ');

SELECT id, normalized_name
FROM ch5_projects
WHERE id = 20001;
```

Save the completed `ch5_finalize.sql`:

```sql
SELECT
  count(*) FILTER (WHERE normalized_name IS NULL) AS nulls,
  count(*) FILTER (
    WHERE normalized_name <> ch5_normalize_name(name)
  ) AS mismatches
FROM ch5_projects;

ALTER TABLE ch5_projects
VALIDATE CONSTRAINT ch5_projects_normalized_name_nn;

ALTER TABLE ch5_projects
ALTER COLUMN normalized_name SET NOT NULL;

ALTER TABLE ch5_projects
DROP CONSTRAINT ch5_projects_normalized_name_nn;

CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ch5_projects_normalized_name_idx
ON ch5_projects (normalized_name);

ANALYZE ch5_projects;
```

Run the complete lab:

```bash
docker run --name ch5-computed-column --rm \
  -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres -d postgres:16

until docker exec ch5-computed-column pg_isready -U postgres; do
  sleep 2
done

docker exec -i ch5-computed-column psql \
  -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -U postgres < ch5_normalized_name.sql

while :; do
  rows=$(docker exec ch5-computed-column psql -X -At -U postgres \
    -c 'SELECT ch5_backfill_normalized_names(2000)')
  echo "updated_rows=$rows"
  [ "$rows" -eq 0 ] && break
done

docker exec -i ch5-computed-column psql \
  -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -U postgres < ch5_finalize.sql
docker stop ch5-computed-column
```

The main file reports 20,000 rows to backfill and `new project` for id 20001. The loop then reports ten batches of 2,000 followed by 0; finalization reports zero NULLs and zero mismatches.

The trigger closes the race with live writes by maintaining the new column before the backfill reaches old rows. Bounded transactions limit lock duration, WAL bursts, and vacuum pressure compared with one update of the whole table.
